pt does and they say look we  want our type system to make intuitive  sense we don't want to be incomplete  we're gonna assume that that you're  mostly treating things as read-only and  let you pass them covariantly some  languages like go say we we need to be  sound we also don't want to add  complexity and the overhead of making  you learn about variants and think about  variants all the time so we're just  going to go with invariant and it's  gonna be a little frustrating sometimes  but at least it's easy to give you great  error messages and it's definitely sound  and then some languages will use  underlying language constructs like  mutability right they'll take advantage  of the fact that the language  understands what's mutable what's not  and they can track that through to  understand whether it's sound to pass  something covariantly or invariantly  okay so we've taken a look at some case  studies to wrap up let's go back to  those three questions I posed at the  very beginning of the talk so the first  one is sometimes we're writing some code  and it has a pretty obvious type error  but our type checker doesn't catch it  we run typescript and typescript says  that codes great you're not going to get  any type errors and then at runtime you  get a  Piper and the answer is that what we're  looking for there is more soundness but  that's not the only goal of a type  system that a lot of tight systems also  want to be complete  they want to be fast they want to give  you great error messages they want to be  easy to learn and sometimes they're  willing to sacrifice a little bit of  soundness to let bugs like that slip  through the cracks in order to achieve  those other goals so the next question  is sometimes we write some code and  we're really sure that this code works  we've tested it we're sure that this is  valid code but a type checker is saying  hey you can't do that that's a type  error and what we're looking for there  is more completeness we're looking for a  type system to not point out things that  aren't actually problems but again  that's not the the primary goal of a lot  of type systems and sometimes the type  system might say look we need to be a  little bit less complete here we need to  have a few more false positives we need  to yell at you a couple of times when  it's it's not totally valid for us to do  that in order for us to create a type  system that's more sound and we'll catch  a different class of bugs and then the  last thing is sometimes we're digging  into our Docs or learning about a type  system and we're like man this seems  pretty complex it seems like there's a  lot of incidental complexity it seems  like this is much more complicated than  the comparable thing in something like  go or Java or rust and the answer is  that we don't have that super power that  the languages that had a type system  from day one have of being able to  design the language around the type  system in addition to the other way  around of being able to introduce new  language concepts or new constructs or  new syntax in order to sort of ease some  of these pains and we need to work with  this existing body of code that uses  runtime if statements to filter down the  types we want to understand those those  kinds of idioms and we might need to  make some more concessions there so when  you're designing a type system and in  particular a gradual type system where  you have that last constraint you're  constrained by you know the the existing  language and the existing idioms  sometimes you have to make these  trade-offs you have to pick to be more  sound or more complete and there's not  usually a clear right answer it's going  to depend on what your goals are right  so for flow the goal is catch as many  bugs as possible and there's almost no  cases where flow is going to choose to  be unsound on the other hand typescript  is a little bit more flexible there  there's those places where they're  willing to be uns